      "source": [
        "# 16 Land cover statistics\n",
        "\n",
        "<!---\n",
        "This needs context about whwat we are doing and why.\n",
        "-->\n",
        "\n",
        "## About the data\n",
        "\n",
        "In this lesson we will use two datasets.\n",
        "\n",
        "The first one is [GAP/LANDFIRE National Terrestrial Ecosystems data for 2011 @davidson_gaplandfire_2016](https://www.sciencebase.gov/catalog/item/573cc51be4b0dae0d5e4b0c5), from the [US Geological Survey (USGS)](https://www.usgs.gov). This is a categorical raster with a 30 m x 30 m pixel resolution representing highly thematically detailed land cover map of the U.S. We will access this data through the [Microsoft Planetary Computer (MPC) data catalog](https://planetarycomputer.microsoft.com/dataset/gap). The class names and corresponding codes have been saved to a separete CSV to simplify access in this lesson. Further information on how to access the classes directly from the data are available in the [MPC catalog](https://planetarycomputer.microsoft.com/dataset/gap#Example-Notebook).\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "The second dataset is a shapefile with the perimeters for 2017 California fires. This data was extracted from the CALFIRE's [Historical Wildland Fire Perimeters](https://gis.data.ca.gov/datasets/CALFIRE-Forestry::california-fire-perimeters-all/about) @CALFIRE_FirePerimeters_All.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Fire perimeter preparation\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's start by importing the necessary libraries:"
      ],
      "id": "e29d596d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import os\n",
        "\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "\n",
        "import geopandas as gpd\n",
        "import rioxarray as rioxr\n",
        "from shapely import box\n",
        "\n",
        "import pystac_client\n",
        "import planetary_computer\n",
        "\n",
        "from IPython.display import Image "
      ],
      "id": "f3a4d308",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Then import and select the Thomas Fire within the fire perimeters data:"
      ],
      "id": "f9ecc596"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "fire_perimeters = gpd.read_file(os.path.join('data',\n",
        "                                             'California_Fire_Perimeters_2017',\n",
        "                                             'California_Fire_Perimeters_2017.shp')\n",
        "                                             )\n",
        "thomas_fire = fire_perimeters[fire_perimeters['FIRE_NAME']=='THOMAS']\n",
        "\n",
        "# Examine fire perimeter data\n",
        "print(thomas_fire.crs)\n",
        "thomas_fire.plot()"
      ],
      "id": "5d8b106b",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Catalog search\n",
        "\n",
        "Our goal is to retrieve the National Terrestrial Ecosystems data over the fire perimeter. In the [last lesson](/book/chapters/lesson-16-STAC.qmd) we used GeoJSON-type dictionary to do the catalog search. Here we will use a list `[xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax]` with the coordinate values defining the four corners of the region we want to search data over. Just as the GeoJSON, these coordinates have to be in the EPSG:4326 CRS. \n",
        "\n",
        "Our bounding box will come from the Thomas Fire perimeter. Notice we use method chaining to reproject and extract the bounds."
      ],
      "id": "335418eb"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Create list of bounding box coords for search in 4326 CRS\n",
        "thomas_fire_bbox = list(thomas_fire.to_crs('epsg:4326') \n",
        "                                   .total_bounds\n",
        "                                    )\n",
        "thomas_fire_bbox"
      ],
      "id": "b588626a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Next, we connect to the Microsoft Planetary Computer data catalog and perform the search. The collection ID for the National Terrestrial Ecosystems data is `gap`:"
      ],
      "id": "ba59d81b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Open MPC data catalog\n",
        "catalog = pystac_client.Client.open(\n",
        "    \"https://planetarycomputer.microsoft.com/api/stac/v1\",\n",
        "    modifier=planetary_computer.sign_inplace,\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Search MPC catalog \n",
        "search = catalog.search(collections=['gap'], \n",
        "                        bbox=thomas_fire_bbox)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Retrieve search items\n",
        "items = search.item_collection()\n",
        "print(f\"Returned {len(items)} Items\")\n",
        "items"
      ],
      "id": "8f32802c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "There is a single item in the search, so let's go ahead and select it and view its pre-rendered image:"
      ],
      "id": "3483cb7f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Select unique search item\n",
        "item = items[0]  \n",
        "\n",
        "# Display pre-rendered image\n",
        "Image(url=item.assets['rendered_preview'].href, width=600)"
      ],
      "id": "6ae815eb",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We can see this is a big raster! \n",
        "\n",
        "At this point, we can also import the CSV file with the class labels:"
      ],
      "id": "5f198b25"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "labels = pd.read_csv(os.path.join('data',\n",
        "                                  'GAP_National_Terrestrial_Ecosystems.csv')\n",
        "                                  )\n",
        "labels.head()"
      ],
      "id": "23b55d8c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Noitce that 0 is used as the no-data value for this raster. This will be relevant in a moment.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Explore raster\n",
        "\n",
        "Next, we can go ahead an open the raster:"
      ],
      "id": "5a33ab9f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Access raster data from item\n",
        "lulc = rioxr.open_rasterio(item.assets['data'].href)\n",
        "lulc"
      ],
      "id": "ebf4c96a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Notice that `band` is a dimension of length 1. We can go ahead and \"squeeze\" the raster to simplify it:"
      ],
      "id": "f1b15038"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Remove length 1 dimension (band)\n",
        "lulc = lulc.squeeze().drop_vars('band')\n",
        "print(\"Sizes of dimensions:\", dict(lulc.sizes))"
      ],
      "id": "74b84a47",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Next, let's look at how the raster is locatd with respect to the Thomas Fire perimeter and the CA state boundary:"
      ],
      "id": "1093d962"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Create GeoDataFrame from raster bounding box\n",
        "lulc_bbox = gpd.GeoDataFrame(geometry = [box(*lulc.rio.bounds())],\n",
        "                             crs = lulc.rio.crs)\n",
        "\n",
        "ca = gpd.read_file(os.path.join('data',\n",
        "                                'ca_state_boundary',   \n",
        "                                'ca_state_boundary.shp'))\n",
        "\n",
        "# ------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
        "# Plot raster boundary, fire perimeter, and CA boundary\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n",
        "ca.plot(ax=ax, color='white', edgecolor ='black')\n",
        "\n",
        "# Reproject lulc_bbox and fire perimeter to match CA crs\n",
        "lulc_bbox.to_crs(ca.crs).plot(ax=ax, alpha=0.3)  \n",
        "thomas_fire.to_crs(ca.crs).plot(ax=ax, color='red')\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "3ec6a87b",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We can see the raster covers a big area relative to the fire perimeter. Since we want to calculate the land coverage statistics within the fire perimeter, we will have to clip the raster to this area. \n",
        "\n",
        "## Clip raster to geometry\n",
        "\n",
        "In our [first lesson about rasters](/book/chapters/lesson-15-rioxarray/lesson-15-rioxarray.qmd) we saw how to clip a raster to a rectangular region. In our case, we want to clip the raster exactly to the fire perimeter. Clipping can be a costly operation for such a big raster relative to a detailed geometry. So we will perform the clipping in two steps:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Clip the raster using the fire perimeter bounding box using [`rio.clip_box()`](https://corteva.github.io/rioxarray/html/rioxarray.html#rioxarray.raster_array.RasterArray.clip_box) and then\n",
        "2. Clip the simplified raster to the fire perimeter using [`rio.clip()`](https://corteva.github.io/rioxarray/html/rioxarray.html#rioxarray.raster_array.RasterArray.clip). "
      ],
      "id": "887e7761"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Match CRSs and verify update\n",
        "thomas_fire_match = thomas_fire.to_crs(lulc.rio.crs)\n",
        "assert thomas_fire_match.crs == lulc.rio.crs\n",
        "\n",
        "# Clip large raster to detailed geometry in two steps\n",
        "lulc_step1 = lulc.rio.clip_box(*thomas_fire_match.total_bounds)\n",
        "lulc_step2 = lulc_step1.rio.clip(thomas_fire_match.geometry)  # Produces RuntimeWarning\n",
        "\n",
        "# ------------------------------------------------------\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(8, 4))\n",
        "\n",
        "# Plot the first clipped raster\n",
        "lulc_step1.plot(ax=ax[0])\n",
        "ax[0].set_title(\"Step 1: Clip to Bounding Box\")\n",
        "ax[0].axis(\"off\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Plot the second clipped raster\n",
        "lulc_step2.plot(ax=ax[1])\n",
        "ax[1].set_title(\"Step 2: Clip to Detailed Geometry\")\n",
        "ax[1].axis(\"off\")\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "6f04f160",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Raster no-data values\n",
        "\n",
        "Notice a warning appeared when we clipped the raster. After some investigation, we will find that this `RuntimeWarning` occurs because the `rio.clip()` function tries to replace values outside the fire perimeter geometry with `np.nan`. However, as we previously saw, our raster's data type is `uint16` (16 bits unsigned integer). The cast issue appears since `np.nan` is a float (decimal number) and it cannot be casted as a `uint16`. To make sure the clipping function fills in any pixels with the adequate no-data value, let's manually set it:"
      ],
      "id": "fb8ad326"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "print('Original no-data value: ', lulc.rio.nodata)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Update raster's no-data value\n",
        "lulc = lulc.rio.write_nodata(0)\n",
        "print('Updated no-data value: ', lulc.rio.nodata)"
      ],
      "id": "18cf1450",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This way, the `rio.clip()` function will know what values to assign to pixels outside the fire perimeter. Let's try clipping again, this time using method chaining:"
      ],
      "id": "782b07f6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "lulc_clip = (lulc.rio.clip_box(*thomas_fire_match.total_bounds)\n",
        "                 .rio.clip(thomas_fire_match.geometry)\n",
        "                 )\n",
        "\n",
        "# Examine results\n",
        "lulc_clip.plot()                                  "
      ],
      "id": "4e706884",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Notice no warning came up during the clipping!\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.callout-warning}\n",
        "## Always pay attention to warnings!\n",
        "\n",
        "Warnings are your program's way of saying, \"Something might go wrong here, take a look!\" They may indicate silent failures, package compatibility issues, or potential bugs, amont other issues. Do not ignore warnings! **Addressing warnings is part of writing clean, maintainable code and reflects a professional approach.**\n",
        "\n",
        "These are some steps to handling warnings effectively:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Read and understand them**: Don’t dismiss warnings without understanding their cause.\n",
        "- **Fix or address them**: Modify your code to resolve the warning if possible.\n",
        "- **Suppress only when necessary**: Use tools to suppress warnings only when you're sure they are irrelevant or benign.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "<!--\n",
        "## Save raster\n",
        "\n",
        "This would be a natural point where we'd like to save our raster for future use. That way, we wouldn't need to perform \n",
        "-->\n",
        "\n",
        "## Land cover statistics\n",
        "\n",
        "In the rest of this lesson we will calculate land cover statistics within the Thomas Fire perimeter. The following exercises will guide you through this process:\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.callout-tip}\n",
        "## Exercises\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Use the `numpy` function `np.unique()` to get the number of pixels per class in `lulc_clip`. HINT: check [the `np.unique()` documentation](https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.unique.html) to see what the `return_counts` parameter does and read the last example. \n",
        "\n",
        "2. Create a data frame `pix_counts` with two columns: column one must be the code numbers for the pixels in `lulc_clip` and column two must be the number of pixels corresponding to each code. HINT: [check our class notes on `pandas.DataFrame`s](/book/chapters/lesson-2-series-dataframes.qmd)\n",
        "\n",
        "<!--"
      ],
      "id": "e70f7e9e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "unique_counts = np.unique(lulc_clip, return_counts=True)\n",
        "\n",
        "pix_counts = pd.DataFrame({'code':unique_counts[0], \n",
        "                           'n_pixels':unique_counts[1]})\n",
        "\n",
        "pix_counts = pix_counts[pix_counts['code']!=0]  # Remove no-data pixels"
      ],
      "id": "dc9a88c7",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "-->\n",
        "\n",
        "3. Use the `labels` data frame to add the class names to the codes in the `pix_counts` data frame. Store the resulting data frame as `classes`. \n",
        "\n",
        "<!--"
      ],
      "id": "b6350c92"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "classes = pd.merge(left=labels, \n",
        "                   right=pix_counts, \n",
        "                   on='code')"
      ],
      "id": "da086a22",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "-->\n",
        "\n",
        "5. What *area* within the fire perimeter (in km^2) was estimated to be developed? HINT: what is the raster's resolution? \n",
        "\n",
        "<!--"
      ],
      "id": "80b9656e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "area_per_pixel = 30**2  # In meters^2\n",
        "classes.iloc[43:47].n_pixels.sum() * area_per_pixel / 1000**2"
      ],
      "id": "c2f7e546",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "-->\n",
        "\n",
        "6. Store the total number of pixels within the fire perimeter as a variable `total_pixels`.\n",
        "\n",
        "7. Add the percentage of area covered by each class as a new column `percentage` to the `classes` data frame. Sort the data frame by percentage coverage in descending order.\n",
        "\n",
        "<!--"
      ],
      "id": "bd83c0cb"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "total_pixels = classes.n_pixels.sum()\n",
        "\n",
        "classes['percentage'] = classes.n_pixels/total_pixels * 100\n",
        "classes = classes.sort_values(by='percentage', ascending=False)"
      ],
      "id": "ff54ea7c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "-->\n",
        "\n",
        "8. Create a horizontal bar plot showing the classes with more than 1% land cover in decreasing order. For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "<!--\n",
        "```python\n",
        "classes[classes['percentage']>1].set_index('class_label').percentage.plot(kind='barh')\n",
        "```\n",
        "-->"
      ],
      "id": "5e60f3c7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: false\n",
        "\n",
        "# Filter and set index\n",
        "filtered_classes = classes[classes['percentage']>1].set_index('class_label').sort_values(by='percentage', ascending=True)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Create the horizontal bar plot\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6, 4))  # Adjusted figure size for better proportions\n",
        "\n",
        "# Plot with improved style\n",
        "filtered_classes.percentage.plot(\n",
        "    kind='barh',\n",
        "    ax=ax,\n",
        "    color='skyblue',\n",
        "    edgecolor='black'\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Add an improved title and center it relative to the entire figure\n",
        "fig.suptitle(\n",
        "    'Distribution of Land Cover Within the Thomas Fire Perimeter\\n(Classes > 1% from 2011 USGS National Terrestrial Ecosystems Data)',\n",
        "    fontsize=11,\n",
        "    y=0.98  # Adjust position slightly for spacing\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Add axis labels\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel('Percentage (%)', fontsize=10)\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel('')  # No label for y-axis\n",
        "\n",
        "# Add gridlines for easier interpretation\n",
        "ax.grid(axis='x', linestyle='--', alpha=0.7)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Add percentage values to the bars\n",
        "for i, v in enumerate(filtered_classes.percentage):\n",
        "    ax.text(v + 0.5, i, f\"{v:.1f}%\", va='center', fontsize=10)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Remove top and right edges of the plot\n",
        "ax.spines['top'].set_visible(False)\n",
        "ax.spines['right'].set_visible(False)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Make the axis box wider relative to the figure area\n",
        "fig.subplots_adjust(left=0.25, right=0.95, top=0.85)  # Adjust spacing for the centered title\n",
        "\n",
        "# Show the plot\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "d78166f5",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
